Linux Operations Lab
      This is my hands-on Linux practice lab. I am using Ubuntu to practice basic Linux commands, users and permissions, processes, logs, networking, and troubleshooting.

1. Users and Permissions
      
      Command: whoami
      What it does: Shows the username of the current user.
      What I learned:  
            It helps me check which user I am currently logged in as.

      Command: id
      What it does: Shows the user ID, group ID, and groups of the current user.
      What I learned: 
            It helps me understand the identity and group memberships of a user.

      Command: ls -l
      What it does: Shows files with their permissions, owner, group, size, and other details.
      What I learned: 
            I can use it to check who owns a file and what permissions are assigned.

      Command: chmod 755 script.sh
      What it does: Changes the permissions of a file.
      What I learned: 
            Linux permissions control whether a user can read, write, or execute a file.

      Command: sudo chown user:user file.txt
      What it does: Changes the owner and group of a file.
      What I learned:
             It is useful when a file has the wrong ownership.

      Command: sudo <command>
      What it does: Runs a command with elevated privileges.
      What I learned:
            Some administrative operations require root privileges.

2. Processes

      Command: ps aux
      What it does: Shows running processes on the system.
      What I learned:
            It helps me see which processes are running and their CPU and memory usage.

      Command: ps aux | grep <process>
      What it does: Searches the process list for a particular process.
      What I learned:
            This can be useful when checking whether a specific application or process is running.

      Command: top
      What it does: Displays running processes and system resource usage in real time.
      What I learned:
            I can use it to check CPU and memory usage and identify resource-heavy processes.

      Command: kill <PID>
      What it does: Sends a signal to a process using its process ID (PID).
      What I learned:
            It can be used to stop a process when necessary.

      Command: pgrep <process>
      What it does: Finds the PID of a process by its name.
      What I learned:
            It is useful when I need to find the PID before working with a process.

3. Logs

      Command: ls /var/log
      What it does: Lists the log files and directories under /var/log.
      What I learned:
            Linux stores many system and application logs under /var/log.

      Command: tail /var/log/syslog
      What it does: Shows the last lines of the system log.
      What I learned:
             It is useful for checking recent log messages.

      Command: tail -n 20 /var/log/syslog
      What it does: Shows the last 20 lines of the log file.
      What I learned:
            I can use it when I want to quickly check the most recent log entries.

      Command: grep -i "error" /var/log/syslog
      What it does: Searches the log for messages containing error.
      What I learned:
            grep is useful for finding specific messages when troubleshooting.

      Command: journalctl
      What it does: Displays logs collected by the systemd journal.
      What I learned:
             It is useful for viewing system and service-related logs.

      Command: journalctl -u <service>
      What it does: Shows logs for a specific systemd service.
      What I learned: I can use it to investigate problems with a particular service.

      Command: journalctl -f
      What it does: Displays new journal log entries as they are generated.
      What I learned:
            It is useful when monitoring logs in real time while troubleshooting.

What I am practicing through this lab, I am learning how to:
                Work with Linux users and permissions , Check file ownership and permissions , View and manage processes , Find processes using their names or PIDs , Read system and service logs
and Use logs for basic troubleshooting.
